<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="diagnostics-environment" title="AW2xxx - environment, drivers and configuration">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <p>
        These fourteen are about the run, not the declaration. They report the class file handed
        over, the driver applying it, the manifest and settings read, and the JVM around them. A
        declaration that will not compile is <a href="diagnostics-declaration.topic">AW1xxx</a>.
    </p>

    <table style="header-row">
        <tr>
            <td>Code</td>
            <td>What happened</td>
            <td>What to do</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW2003</code></td>
            <td>Class file below major version 50</td>
            <td>Recompile the target; it went unwoven</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW2004</code></td>
            <td>Preview class file, wrong JDK version</td>
            <td>Compile it where it will run</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW2101</code></td>
            <td>Structural weave hits a loaded class</td>
            <td>Weave at build time instead</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW2201</code></td>
            <td>Build-time input another plan wove</td>
            <td>Build clean, or weave the originals</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW2202</code></td>
            <td>Load-time class woven at build time</td>
            <td>Drop one driver; both plans apply</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW2300</code></td>
            <td>A manifest that cannot be trusted</td>
            <td>Rebuild the artefact holding it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW2301</code></td>
            <td>Manifest schema newer than this build</td>
            <td>Update %product%, or rebuild it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW2302</code></td>
            <td>No manifest anywhere on the classpath</td>
            <td>Add the processor to that module</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW2303</code></td>
            <td>Two artefacts declare one weave class</td>
            <td>Remove a dependency, or rename one</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW2310</code></td>
            <td>A setting was not understood</td>
            <td>Check the key, and the value it lists</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW2401</code></td>
            <td>Weaving class loader, AOT cache on</td>
            <td>Weave at build time instead</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW2402</code></td>
            <td>A module graph was widened for you</td>
            <td>Nothing, unless the JVM refused</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW2403</code></td>
            <td>Reserved; no code reports it</td>
            <td>Nothing to do</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>AW2501</code></td>
            <td>Dependency classes written elsewhere</td>
            <td>Put that directory on the classpath</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        Six of them are errors: <code>AW2003</code>, <code>AW2004</code>, <code>AW2101</code>,
        <code>AW2201</code>, <code>AW2300</code> and <code>AW2301</code>. <code>AW2402</code> is
        info, and the other seven are warnings. Whether an error stops you is the driver's rule,
        and <a href="diagnostics.topic">Diagnostics</a> has all three.
    </p>

    <chapter title="A manifest that cannot be trusted" id="manifest">
        <p>
            <code>AW2300</code> is the broadest code in the range, and twelve places report it.
            Which of them you hit decides what to change.
            <a href="manifest-format.topic">The weave manifest</a> has the document's layout.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="The document is damaged">
                <p>Three shapes of damage, one remedy — rebuild the artefact — though one of the
                    three reports with no remedy of its own:</p>
                <list>
                    <li><p>JSON that does not parse.</p></li>
                    <li><p>An entry naming no class, no handler or no target method — reported
                        with no remedy of its own.</p></li>
                    <li><p>An <a href="extension-methods.topic">extension</a> entry missing one of
                        the four parts that identify it.</p></li>
                </list>
                <p>
                    A manifest is generated, so a broken one was hand-edited or truncated in
                    transit.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A token this release does not know">
                <p>
                    An extension whose <code>kind</code> is unknown is dropped, because that field
                    decides how a call site is rewritten. Update %product%, or rebuild the
                    artefact against this version. An unknown <code>require</code>,
                    <code>nulls</code> or <code>scope</code> keeps the entry and takes the default
                    instead; update %product% only if that policy needs enforcing here.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The file would not open">
                <list>
                    <li><p>The classpath itself could not be searched. The whole run yields no
                        candidates, and it is reported twice — <code>AW2300</code> here, and
                        <code>AW2302</code> as well, whose "add the processor" remedy does not fit
                        this cause.</p></li>
                    <li><p>One manifest could not be read. Only that root is lost, discovery still
                        reads the rest, and this carries no remedy of its own.</p></li>
                </list>
            </def>
            <def title="A class its manifest names is gone">
                <p>
                    A runtime driver reads each weave out of the artefact that declared it, never
                    off the wider classpath. Rebuild that artefact; its manifest outlived the class.
                    <a href="weave-discovery.topic">How a weave is found</a> has the search order.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The processor could not write one">
                <p>
                    Raised by the annotation processor rather than by any driver, so it reaches you
                    as a compiler message. Check that the processor can write to the compilation
                    output directory. <a href="install.topic">Install</a> has the wiring it needs.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <note>
            <p>
                Three faults produce no code at all: a member with a blank name, an injection
                point given but blank, and a version that narrows below zero. A manifest
                carrying any of them parses, then throws <code>IllegalArgumentException</code>
                out of <code>ManifestReader.read</code>. Discovery ends there, with the later
                manifests unread. Rebuild that artefact too.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Woven twice, or loaded too early" id="second-pass">
        <p>
            Three codes say the class was not fresh. The first two come from one check: a weave
            record whose fingerprint is not this plan's. Only the driver tells them apart.
            <a href="determinism.topic">Determinism</a> has what a fingerprint covers.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="A build-time input was woven before">
                <p>
                    Reported when the driver is <code>BUILD</code> — the Maven plugin, and the
                    testkit, which never sets a driver and defaults to it too. The class is
                    refused and left as it arrived, so a second plan cannot stack on the first.
                    Run a clean build. Where the input is an artefact that has been through
                    weaving already, such as a shaded jar, weave the original classes instead.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A load-time class was woven at build time">
                <p>
                    Reported when the driver is <code>LOAD</code> — the agent, and the weaving
                    class loader. Giving the agent and the plugin the same weaves keeps the two
                    plans' fingerprints equal, and the class is left alone with no diagnostic.
                    Any other weave list reports <code>AW2202</code> instead: both plans apply,
                    but only a weave they genuinely share fires twice.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Under <code>-javaagent</code> a report raised after install may never reach
                    you: <a href="diagnostics.topic">Diagnostics</a> covers when the agent drains
                    its listener. <a href="choose-a-driver.topic">Choose a driver</a> compares
                    the three.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A structural weave meets a loaded class">
                <p>
                    Reached only from a dynamic attach, since <code>-javaagent</code> weaves
                    every target as it loads and never meets an already-loaded one. The agent
                    reports <code>AW2101</code> once per weave that would change an
                    already-loaded class's shape. That covers adding or removing a member, and
                    clearing <code>final</code> from a <code>@Shadow</code> field the weave
                    writes to. A separate check retransforms only the classes left with no such
                    change, and reports <code>AW2101</code> again if the JVM still refuses.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Nothing is lost either way: the transformer stays installed, and classes
                    loading from then on are woven in full, this weave included. Weave at build
                    time, or start the JVM with <code>-javaagent</code> so the targets are woven
                    as they load.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Settings that never took effect" id="settings">
        <p>
            <code>AW2310</code> is one code for five ways a setting fails to apply. No value is
            ever coerced, so the setting is left for a lower-precedence layer rather than guessed
            at. Five things raise it:
        </p>

        <list>
            <li><p>A key nothing recognises. The nearest known key is offered where there is
                one — paste it back.</p></li>
            <li><p>An agent argument with no <code>=</code>. Write comma-separated
                <code>key=value</code> pairs.</p></li>
            <li><p>A boolean that is neither <code>true</code> nor <code>false</code>. Nothing
                reads as a deliberate off.</p></li>
            <li><p>A number that does not parse. Give a decimal integer.</p></li>
            <li><p>An enumerated setting given a value it does not take. The diagnostic lists
                every value it does.</p></li>
        </list>

        <p>
            <a href="configuration-keys.topic">Configuration keys</a> has the keys and their precedence, and
            <a href="agent-options.topic">Agent options</a> the argument spelling.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="The JVM and the build around it" id="around">
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="A weaving class loader under an AOT cache">
                <p>
                    The loader reports <code>AW2401</code> once per construction, never per class.
                    Three flags raise it: <code>-XX:AOTCache=</code>, <code>-XX:AOTCacheOutput=</code> and
                    <code>-XX:AOTConfiguration=</code>. <code>-XX:AOTMode=off</code> beside any of
                    them cancels the report, and <code>-XX:SharedArchiveFile</code> is deliberately
                    left out. Cached classes load eagerly, so a target a parent loader also sees is
                    probably defined, unwoven, first. Weave at build time and the two stop
                    competing.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A module graph the agent widened">
                <p>
                    <code>AW2402</code> depends on the agent's own module, not the weave class's:
                    it fires only where the module <code>WeaverAgent</code> was loaded into is
                    named. Under an ordinary <code>-javaagent</code> run that module is unnamed,
                    so nothing is reported either way, and a refused widening throws
                    <code>IllegalAccessError</code> with no diagnostic ahead of it.
                </p>
                <p>
                    On the classpath the JVM grants the edge itself; it is reported on success
                    too, because widening what an application may reach should leave a trace.
                    Put the weave class on the classpath instead.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Dependency classes written beside your own">
                <p>
                    The Maven plugin reports <code>AW2501</code> once at the end of a run that wove
                    dependency artefacts, listing every class it touched. The originals are untouched, so
                    deleting the directory undoes all of it. Nothing has put it on a classpath yet:
                    set it ahead of the dependency jars wherever your build assembles one.
                    <a href="build-time-weaving.topic#dependencies">Weaving dependencies</a> has the goal.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A number held against reuse">
                <p>
                    <code>AW2403</code> has no reporting site and no build produces it. The constant
                    exists so that an unrelated condition cannot take the number. There is nothing
                    to do about it, and the same holds for the four reserved numbers in
                    <a href="diagnostics-declaration.topic">AW1xxx</a> and
                    <a href="diagnostics-verification.topic">AW4xxx</a>.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

